WEBVTT

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.500
This, to my mind, is one of the most remarkable photographs that's ever been taken of a lion.

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:40.830
It shows Elsa, the famous lioness that was reared by Joy and George Adamson.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.710
She had just killed this buffalo, killed it for herself.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:52.130
And yet at this moment, when all her most powerful instincts of savagery, the instincts of a hunter, were aroused,

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:59.640
she allowed these two men to drag her prey, her kill from her, and yet do nothing whatever to prevent them.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.650
She thus proved that she was a lioness of two worlds.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:07.310
A lioness who could live in the world of the wild savage bush.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:09.730
and also in the world of human beings.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:16.450
Her story starts several years ago when George Adamson brought back three young orphan cubs.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:18.300
George and Joy between them.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.460
We add the three.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:23.070
and when they became too large to be convenient household pets.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.310
Two of them were sent to European zoos.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:26.590
But Elsa...

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.080
Their favorite, they kept.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:29.990
And they're determined to try.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.120
and return her.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.160
to the life of a wild lioness in the bush.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:35.220
but this

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.010
wasn't as easy as you might think.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:38.520
For they had to teach her.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:39.940
how to stalk her prey.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:41.770
and how to hunt and how to kill.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:44.310
how they succeeded in doing so.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.130
Joy Adamson told in her book.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:46.930
born free.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.930
But eventually, they did release Elsa.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.030
in the wild and remote part of Kenya.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:53.720
Every now and again,

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.560
They revisited her and announced their arrival with a rifle shot.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:58.930
And soon afterwards,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:01:59.720
Elsa.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:00.540
was appeared.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.280
And on one occasion,

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:03.220
One cheer.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:04.020
appeared.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:05.390
She brought with her proof.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.100
that she really had become.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.830
are a wild lioness in the full sense of the world.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.880
for she brought with her

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.510
three small cubs.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:15.660
No, sadly.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:16.560
Elsa?

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:17.360
is dead.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:20.670
But a few months ago, before she died,

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.740
My cameraman colleague, Geoffrey Mulligan and I,

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.320
were fortunate enough to be invited by the Adamson's out to Kenya.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.380
to see Elsa and her cubs.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.220
We arrived...

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:32.840
at the end of the dry season.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:34.150
when the whole of Kenya

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.740
was dry and bare and dusty.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:41.600
Here, in the north,

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:44.040
The country is covered by thick spiny bush.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:48.010
all of it at this time of the year, quite leafless, except by the river.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.480
where the trees, nourished by a permanent supply of water,

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:53.920
can grow tall and we're in full leaf.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:58.350
It was in this green oasis that the Adamstons had made their permanent camp.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:02:59.860
George Adams.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.080
is Senior Game Warden of the Northern Frontier Province,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.280
a vast area of over 120,000 square miles.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.720
And here, by the river, he has a good opportunity of observing games.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.650
for animals come from many miles around to drink.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:19.150
Little spotted doves were always sipping from the riverside pools.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:28.380
And this pack of baboons were regular visitors coming each evening not only to drink,

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:29.880
but to dig for roots.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:30.780
in the soft earth.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:31.850
of the riverbanks.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:38.500
On the opposite side,

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:39.830
in the shade of the trees.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.900
Joy Adamson had her studio.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.270
For she herself is an artist.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:46.960
whose beautiful drawings of African plants

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.370
and of costumes of some of the Kenya tribes.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.850
are displayed in the museum in Nairobi.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:53.990
And as she sat in her studio,

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.320
she could watch the procession of visitors.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:58.930
who unknowingly posed for her.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:37.880
Elsa herself often came to this river to drink, and as we watched the baboons, we were hoping that she might arrive on this evening.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.510
But we couldn't be sure that she would come.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:49.300
In fact, she had not visited the camp for several days past, and she might be far away in the bush, hunting to provide food for her cubs.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:54.270
When evening came, she still had not appeared.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.410
But that night, as we lay in our tents, we heard her.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:04:59.910
But the roars were not her normal call.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:24.910
she was fighting with a strange lioness whose tracks george had seen not far from camp and the battle sounded a desperate one it might well be that elsa fighting to defend her territory had been badly injured in the battle and was lying up somewhere in need of help so the next morning the adamson set out to look for her

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.140
She might be anywhere in the surrounding wild country,

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:40.140
and the task of locating her seemed almost impossible.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:43.290
But George had lived in Kenya for most of his life,

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:48.120
and for him the dusty ground was a book full of information which could be easily read.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:50.760
Here, a lizard had left a trail.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:54.750
Birds and other small creatures had recently passed this way too,

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:57.090
but there were no signs of a lion.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:09.700
From the thorn trees, little bush babies watched the Adamson's as they searched.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:29.970
They had thought that...

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.500
Elsa might have headed for a large tree, which recently had been one of her favourite haunts,

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:36.760
where she slept during the heat of the day.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.650
But they could see no sign whatever of her tracks in that direction.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:46.060
George was sure that she must have gone some other way after the battle.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:59.980
Anxiously they searched for hour after hour as the sun grew hotter and the day wore on.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:10.930
And then, at last, they found Elsa's spoor.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.360
The tracks led towards a huge rock mountain,

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.760
close by the place where Elsa had given birth to her cubs.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:21.500
George judged from the tracks that Elsa was limping.

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:22.960
She must be injured.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:27.260
Perhaps she was lying up, stiff and sore, quite close by,

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:29.970
having been unable to get as far as her favourite.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:40.160
Come on Elsa! Come on Elsa! Come on Elsa!

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:12.010
Come on Elsa, come on Elsa, come on Elsa.

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:35.880
But when, at last, the rock came into sight,

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.920
there was no sign of her in her favourite position

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:40.500
basking in the sun on the summit.

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:49.010
There was no doubt from her limp that she was injured.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:57.390
It seemed incredible that this powerful creature, which only the previous night had been fighting

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:59.130
with all her savagery and strength.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.210
living free and unfettered in the bush was now responding to the call of a human foster mother.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:34.780
Machi, Machi, let's come back, George, George.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:07.600
Two of the cubs, whom Joy called Timid One and Little Elsa,

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:12.100
kept their distance, unsure about the human being with whom their mother was so intimate.

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:16.310
But the third cub, Jesper, regarded himself as his mother's bodyguard.

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:17.970
Come on, Jesper!

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:19.330
Jesper, no, no, no!

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.230
No, no.

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:41.760
Jesper had always taken this protective attitude towards his mother

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:44.160
ever since he had been a really tiny cub.

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:49.020
And unlike his brother and sister, it was seldom that he left Elsa's side.

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:02.360
Come on, Jesper!

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:05.820
Come on, come on, come on!

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.610
Come on, goody, goody!

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:09.930
Come on, chocolate!

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:12.500
Niamma, niamma, chocolate!

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:15.480
Elsa, come on, come on, Els!

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:17.680
Come on, goody, goody, Els!

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:19.300
Come on, come on!

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:59.840
when joy at last managed to examine elsa closely she discovered that there were deep wounds both on elsa's left forepaw and on her hind legs where she had been bitten by the rival lioness during

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:16.110
the battle. Clearly it would be several days before she would be able to hunt

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:22.630
again and provide food for the cubs. Meanwhile they would go hungry. It was

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:27.020
important therefore to persuade her to come back to camp where she could be

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:29.860
treated and she and the cubs could be fed.

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:38.050
But would she come, injured as she was?

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:49.220
Certainly, the cubs would not come without her.

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:17.720
Jesper.

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:18.640
The Faithful.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:20.420
received a mother's thanks.

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:40.990
Bye-bye.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:45.910
Now,

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:47.190
As she descended,

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:51.660
it was easy to see how much Elsa's wounds were handicapping her.

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:54.760
They were obviously paining her a great deal.

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:59.960
and it might well be that she would be unwilling to undertake the long walk through.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:00.480
the bush.

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:01.480
back to camp.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:55.260
even when Elsa had at last limped to the bottom of the rock.

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:56.330
Timid one.

00:15:56.620 --> 00:15:57.880
Seemed unwilling to leave.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:15:59.440
and lingered at the top.

00:15:59.720 --> 00:15:59.960
With.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:07.040
his sister, little Elsa. Neither of them were in any way tame or in any degree under the

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:29.320
Adamson's control. But Jesper, as always, was by his mother's side.

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:59.300
It was over a mile back to camp, and Elsa had to stop for frequent rests.

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:08.600
then joy and george caressed her and encouraged her to continue while little elsa and timid one

00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:27.270
watched nervously from the bushes even now jesper was by no means sure of joy's intentions

